# Broker operations grant — the CLUSTER-SCOPED half of 06 §2.2.1. THE INSTALL-PATH COPY.
#
# Rendered by `render_broker_operations_grant` in common.sh and applied by every step that installs
# an agent tier. `examples/gitops-repo/policy/rbac-overlay/broker-operations.yaml` is the reference
# copy a human reads; this is the one that lands on a cluster. Both are held to 06 §2.2.1 itself by
# `dev/tests/actor-grant-single-sourced.py` (V-BRK-013), in both directions — neither is checked
# against the other, because two copies that agree with each other and disagree with the spec is the
# failure mode a copy-to-copy check cannot see.
#
# ── THE NAMESPACED HALF IS RETIRED (P9-T9b-5b-0-ii-b), THIS ONE IS NOT ─────────────────────────
# This file used to carry a second document: a tier-neutral `Role kubeagents-broker-operations` with
# the journal and the approval roster, bound per namespace from agent-identity.yaml.template. Every
# triple of it now lands in the per-tier `actor-grant-<tier>.yaml.template` beside this one, stamped
# `kube-agents/tier`, because V-BRK-013 property 4 holds the UNION OF A TIER'S OBJECTS to that tier's
# profile and an unstamped shared object belongs to every tier and is therefore evidence for none.
# Two objects granting one thing is also the drift surface this whole check exists to close.
#
# The ClusterRole below survives that argument, and the reason is the developer-team tier. 06 §2.2
# makes its template a `Role` and `vap-agent-readonly` validation 2 denies a ClusterRole labelled
# `kube-agents/tier: developer-team`, so the three cluster-scoped kinds here — `tokenreviews`,
# `fleetfreezes`, `changepolicies` — cannot be granted to that tier by any object the tier may own.
# A namespace-scoped tier needs a cluster-scoped grant it does not own, and the only admissible one
# is an object wearing no tier label at all. Losing this is not a narrowing: 06 §2.2.1 says a tier
# that cannot read `fleetfreezes` fails closed permanently (06 §4.4), which bricks it.
#
# On a cluster provisioned before that change the old Role and its RoleBinding are still standing.
# They grant exactly what the tier Role now grants, so they are stale rather than dangerous;
# `delete_agent_identity` removes the binding by its old name, and the Role is left as the fleet
# object it always was.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Until P9-T7d-5 the grant existed only under examples/, and nothing on
# the install path applied it. Every actor identity the broker resolves by name would have been
# absent from a freshly provisioned cluster: the broker authenticates its caller with a TokenReview
# it has no permission to create, and fails closed on step 1 of its own pipeline. That is LSN-039 —
# the reference tree is not an install path, and a manifest nobody applies is a design document with
# YAML syntax.
#
# TIER-NEUTRAL BY SPECIFICATION. One ClusterRole and one Role *shape* for the whole fleet; each tier
# binds to them from `agent-identity.yaml.template`. There is deliberately no `kube-agents/tier`
# label here — stamping one on a shared object would be a lie the ValidatingAdmissionPolicy would
# then enforce. `kube-agents/role: actor` is what selects it into `vap-agent-readonly`'s actor arm,
# which is why that policy's match condition tests for either label rather than for tier alone.
#
# THE SPLIT IS THE API'S OWN SEAM. tokenreviews, fleetfreezes and changepolicies are cluster-scoped
# and cannot be granted by a namespaced Role; actionrecords, actionrecords/status and approvalrosters
# are namespaced and now live in the per-tier Role. The split is chosen so that every verb in the
# grant that changes PERSISTED state lands in the namespaced half: an actor appends to the journal in
# its own namespace and nowhere else. `create tokenreviews` is the one write verb here, and it stores
# nothing.
#
# NOT HERE, DELIBERATELY: `update` and `delete` on `actionrecords`. The broker appends and advances
# `status`; it can never rewrite or remove a record, including its own (06 §2.2.1, 03 §3.3 rule 4).
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: kubeagents-broker-operations
  labels:
    kube-agents/role: actor
rules:
  # step 1: authenticate the calling agent. Creates nothing; TokenReview is a request object.
  # Not `system:auth-delegator`, which also confers subjectaccessreviews — this grant must be
  # EXACTLY 06 §2.2.1 and V-BRK-013 fails on a superset as readily as on a subset.
  - apiGroups: ["authentication.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["tokenreviews"]
    verbs: ["create"]
  # step 5: the brake. MUST be readable by every tier — a tier that cannot read the freeze object
  # fails closed permanently (06 §4.4), so omitting this does not fail safe, it bricks the tier.
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["fleetfreezes"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  # step 4: classification input.
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["changepolicies"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  # steps 3 and 5: the Agent set. Read-only, and CLUSTER-SCOPED deliberately.
  #
  # 06 §2.2 appends this grant "verbatim to every actor Role/ClusterRole", and for the platform
  # and cluster-admin tiers that object is a ClusterRole -- so the spec grants this read at
  # cluster scope. It used to sit in the namespaced half below, on the reading that agents is a
  # namespaced kind. That is true of the kind and false of the requirement: `LowerTierOwner`
  # answers "which Agent's SCOPE covers namespace X", which cannot be found by listing in X,
  # so it lists cluster-wide on every namespaced operation and `resolveOwner` guards it with
  # nothing but `live != nil`. Granted namespaced, the ownership gate failed closed for every
  # envelope on every cluster, and nothing below L2 could see it -- `broker-execute-l2.sh` was
  # the first thing to submit a real envelope at a deployed broker and it stopped here.
  #
  # Moving it does not widen the grant: the (apiGroup, resource, verb) set is unchanged, which
  # is why V-BRK-013 stays green across this edit. Nor does it touch the split's actual rule --
  # "every verb that changes PERSISTED state lands in the namespaced half" -- because get, list
  # and watch change nothing. The two verbs that do write, `create actionrecords` and the status
  # update, stay below, bound by a RoleBinding, for the reason stated there.
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["agents"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
